      "title": "Mourinho receives 'eternal glory' from Roma fans on return with Real Madrid",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/sports/football-news/mourinho-receives-eternal-glory-from-roma-fans-on-return-with-real-madrid-126101500177_1.html",
      "description": "Jose Mourinho was treated like a returning hero when he brought Real Madrid to face Roma, one of his many former clubs, on Wednesday.\nAnd he snatched the victory, too, as Madrid came back from two goals down for a 4-3 win with three goals in the final 10 minutes - with all three scores following corner kicks.\nEven before the coach walked out into the Stadio Olimpico ahead of kickoff for a Champions League match, there was a long applause when the stadium announcer read out the teams and Mourinho's name.\nThen early in the first half, the end of the stadium where Roma's hard-core \"ultra\" fans sit held up three huge signs featuring images of Mourinho - one of which depicted him cradling the Conference League trophy that Roma won in 2022 to conclude his first season in charge.\nThe Conference title was Roma's first significant European trophy.\nMourinho also led Roma to the Europa League final in 2023. He left the club in January 2024 without really saying goodbye.\n\"Whoever brings glor",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-15T06:08:38"
    },
    {
      "title": "Vinicius handed four-game ban; set to miss Clasico after hair-pulling row",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/sports/football-news/vinicius-handed-four-game-ban-set-to-miss-clasico-after-hair-pulling-row-126101500163_1.html",
      "description": "Real Madrid forward Vinicius Junior has been banned for four league matches, including the \"clasico\" against Barcelona, after pulling the hair of an opponent in Saturday's 1-0 win over Villarreal.\nVinicius was shown a straight red card in the 73rd minute for having tugged hard on the hair of Villarreal's Nathan Saliba when they were sprinting down the field, moments before the Madrid player scored a goal. The goal was also ruled out.\nThe Spanish soccer federation's disciplinary committee announced the four-match suspension shortly before Madrid's Champions League match at Roma on Wednesday. Madrid came from two goals down to snatch a 4-3 win in the Italian capital and coach Jose Mourinho slammed the timing of the announcement afterwards.\n\"Do you think it's normal for a player from a Spanish team, preparing for an important match that's also crucial for Spanish soccer, to find out about his four-match suspension just two hours before kickoff?\" Mourinho said.\n\"Couldn't they have ...",
